// Dynamic sequence view: MIMI consent round-trip before first contact with a remote user.
//
// STATUS: WIP. This flow tracks draft-ietf-mimi-protocol's /requestConsent + /updateConsent
// endpoints. Both the MIMI spec and Velox's implementation of it are work-in-progress —
// endpoint shapes, payload fields, retry semantics, and revocation behaviour may change.
//
// Why consent exists:
//   - MIMI treats unsolicited KeyPackage release as an anti-abuse / privacy concern.
//     Without consent, a remote instance could enumerate users on a peer by asking for
//     KeyPackages. Consent is the MIMI-mandated gate before /keyMaterial is allowed.
//
// Triggers:
//   - First attempt to add a remote user to a chat, where the local consent cache for
//     (local inviter, remote user/domain) is empty or expired.
//   - An explicit "request chat" / "start DM" UX action targeting a remote user.
//
// Outcome: Identity on the peer records a consent decision; peer pushes /updateConsent
// back to us; local Identity writes the consent token; the original operation (typically
// the add-member call) retries automatically.

views {
  dynamic view flowFederatedConsent {
    title "Flows / Federated Consent (MIMI, WIP)"
    description '''
      First-contact consent round-trip between two Velox instances using MIMI's
      `/requestConsent` + `/updateConsent` endpoints. This flow runs BEFORE a remote
      KeyPackage can be fetched (see flow-federated-member-add for what happens once
      consent is on file).

      ---

      **Consent (WIP, MIMI)** — Per-user, per-requester permission to release a KeyPackage.
      Bob (on otherVelox) must approve Alice's (on velox) request before velox can retrieve
      Bob's KeyPackage from otherVelox. Without this, otherVelox's Federation Edge refuses
      the `/keyMaterial` call with HTTP 403.

      **/requestConsent/{targetDomain} (WIP)** — MIMI endpoint on the TARGET user's instance.
      Carries the requester's identity, the target user's identifier, and the stated purpose
      (e.g. "add to chat {roomId}"). Peer Identity records the request and surfaces it in
      the target user's UI.

      **/updateConsent/{requesterDomain} (WIP)** — MIMI endpoint on the REQUESTER's instance.
      Peer Identity calls it to convey the user's decision (granted / denied / revoked).
      Requester Identity writes the consent token to Identity Postgres, keyed by
      (local-user, remote-user-or-domain).

      **Consent token** — Opaque blob returned in the /updateConsent payload. Cached by the
      local Identity so subsequent MIMI calls can skip re-prompting until the token expires
      or is revoked.

      **mimi:// URIs (WIP)** — MIMI's canonical identifier scheme
      (`mimi://domain/u/user`). Velox is migrating toward these for all cross-instance
      identifiers; until then, legacy Velox identifiers travel alongside in a compat field.

      **Spec status (WIP)** — MIMI is currently draft-ietf-mimi-protocol-05 (Oct 2025), an
      IETF Internet-Draft. Endpoint names, payload encoding (TLS presentation language), and
      lifecycle semantics may change before publication.
    '''
    variant sequence

    // Alice initiates: "add Bob to chat" hits a consent wall because we have no token.
    velox.webUI -> velox.apiService "POST /chats/{id}/members (remote user bob@otherVelox)"
    velox.apiService -> velox.identityService "consent check for (alice, bob@otherVelox)"
    velox.identityService -> velox.identityPostgres "lookup consent token"
    velox.apiService <- velox.identityService "no token — initiate consent request"
    velox.webUI <- velox.apiService "202 Accepted — consent pending"

    // Velox asks otherVelox for consent on Bob's behalf.
    velox.apiService -> velox.interconnectService "POST /requestConsent/{otherVelox} (MIMI — WIP)"
    velox.interconnectService -> dns "SRV + .well-known (MIMI directory path — WIP)"
    velox.interconnectService <- dns "peer Federation Edge endpoint + ed25519 signing key"
    velox.interconnectService -> otherVelox "POST /requestConsent — mTLS + ed25519-signed"
    velox.interconnectService <- otherVelox "202 Accepted — request queued for Bob"

    // Peer surfaces the request to Bob; Bob decides. (That happens on otherVelox — opaque from here.)
    velox.interconnectService -> velox.interconnectService "wait for peer-initiated /updateConsent callback"

    // Peer calls us back via our Federation Edge with Bob's decision.
    otherVelox -> velox.federationEdge "POST /updateConsent/{velox} (bob granted, consent token) — mTLS + ed25519"
    velox.federationEdge -> velox.federationEdge "verify peer ed25519 signature"
    velox.federationEdge -> velox.identityService "route /updateConsent → Identity (MIMI — WIP)"
    velox.identityService -> velox.identityPostgres "store consent token for (alice, bob@otherVelox)"
    velox.federationEdge <- velox.identityService "OK"
    otherVelox <- velox.federationEdge "200 OK (ed25519-signed ACK)"

    // Now the original add-member flow can proceed automatically.
    velox.identityService -[async]-> velox.apiService "consent granted → resume pending add-member"
    velox.apiService -> velox.webUI "push: consent granted for bob@otherVelox — add can now proceed"
    // Flow continues as flow-federated-member-add (/keyMaterial fetch, Commit + Welcome, …).
  }
}
